1
00:00:00,031 --> 00:00:04,075
although I did try it on this original gadget and it was okay.

2
00:00:06,078 --> 00:00:09,663
Yeah, so I'll try and keep an eye on the chat just in case something comes up.

3
00:00:12,125 --> 00:00:15,048
Okay.

4
00:00:16,070 --> 00:00:16,710
Good evening.

5
00:00:16,751 --> 00:00:19,614
I trust you had a fruitful day.

6
00:00:20,074 --> 00:00:23,118
I don't say happy, although I hope it's been happy.

7
00:00:23,925 --> 00:00:53,189
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato samasambuddhasa Namo tassa bhagavato arahato samasambuddhasa Namo tassa bhagavato arahato samasambuddhasa Homage to the Buddha, the blessed, noble and fully self-awakened one.

8
00:00:56,090 --> 00:01:01,875
So I moved on to a different discourse now, and I won't tell you the name because it's part of the discourse.

9
00:01:02,634 --> 00:01:08,159
It all begins with this fellow called Dandapani.

10
00:01:08,219 --> 00:01:08,661
Where is he?

11
00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:10,001
Here we are, Dandapani.

12
00:01:10,802 --> 00:01:22,912
He also is a Sakyan like the Buddha, belongs to the same clan just below, well, what's present day, the border of Nepal and India.

13
00:01:22,932 --> 00:01:25,995
And he's walking and wandering for exercise in the Great Wood.

14
00:01:26,515 --> 00:01:37,924
And he sees the Blessed One, exchanges greetings, and then leaning, he stood to his side, leaning on his stick, he asked the Blessed One, what does the recluse assert?

15
00:01:38,105 --> 00:01:39,447
What does he proclaim?

16
00:01:40,087 --> 00:01:41,989
So there's no gentility about this.

17
00:01:42,108 --> 00:01:46,313
Normally it's the Blessed One or the recluse Gautama.

18
00:01:46,733 --> 00:01:50,337
He just comes out, as it were, quite direct.

19
00:01:51,197 --> 00:01:55,981
And the Buddha says, the Buddha answers, I assert and proclaim such

20
00:01:56,283 --> 00:02:24,314
Such a teaching that one does not quarrel with anyone in the world, with its gods, its Maharas, its Brahmas, in this generation, with its recluses and Brahmins, its princes and its people, such that perceptions no more underlie that Brahmin who abides detached from sensual pleasures, without perplexity, shorn of worry,

21
00:02:24,953 --> 00:02:28,897
free from craving of any kind of becoming.

22
00:02:30,580 --> 00:02:41,032
Okay, so Dandapani shook his head, wagged his tongue and raised his eyebrows until his forehead was puckered in three lines.

23
00:02:41,772 --> 00:02:44,876
Then he departed leaning on his stick.

24
00:02:46,397 --> 00:02:49,181
Dandapani is a nickname, it means stick in hand.

25
00:02:50,302 --> 00:02:52,344
Now, the commentaries say

26
00:02:52,679 --> 00:03:03,393
that he supported Devadatta, who was the renegade monk who tried to kill the Buddha and set up his own order, and eventually set up his own order.

27
00:03:03,713 --> 00:03:07,878
I'm sure you all know that story.

28
00:03:07,899 --> 00:03:17,792
So now, when it was evening, the Blessed One rose from meditation and went to Nigrodha's park, where he sat down on a seat made ready for him, and there were some bhikkhus there.

29
00:03:19,274 --> 00:03:23,018
And they asked him, what is it?

30
00:03:23,198 --> 00:03:25,180
Because they must have heard this answer.

31
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:35,789
Venerable sir, what is the teaching that the Blessed One asserts, whereby one does not quarrel with anyone in the world, with its princes and its people, etc.?

32
00:03:36,550 --> 00:03:47,121
How is it that perceptions no more underlie that Brahmin who abides detached from sensual pleasures, without perplexity, shorn of worry,

33
00:03:47,472 --> 00:03:51,157
free from craving for any kind of being or becoming.

34
00:03:53,600 --> 00:04:05,598
So here we have, just remember that phrase there, that perceptions no more underlie the Brahmi.

35
00:04:05,618 --> 00:04:12,288
So here the Brahmi means somebody who's liberated, who abides detached from sensual pleasures, etc.

36
00:04:14,711 --> 00:04:15,993
And the Buddha answers,

37
00:04:16,497 --> 00:04:45,339
as to the source through which perceptions and notions tinged by mental proliferation beset a man, if nothing is found there to delight in, welcome and hold onto, this is the end of the underlying tendency to lust, of the underlying tendency to aversion, of the underlying tendency to views, to doubt,

38
00:04:45,589 --> 00:05:11,125
to conceit to desire for becoming to ignorance this is the end of resorting to rods and weapons of quarrels brawls disputes recrimination malice and false speech here these evil unwholesome states cease without remainder now that is what the blessed one said and having said that

39
00:05:12,403 --> 00:05:37,786
sublime one rose from his seat and went into his dwelling and soon after that uh these uh monks said to themselves uh the buddhas the the blessed one has gone to his dwelling after giving a summary and brief without expounding the detailed meaning now who will expound it for us and then they think of mahakachana mahakachana was also fully liberated

40
00:05:38,240 --> 00:05:49,901
And he was nominated by the Buddha or given the title by the Buddha of foremost in expounding the subtleties of the Dharma.

41
00:05:49,920 --> 00:05:55,029
So they go to see Mahakachana and exchange greetings, the usual plenaries.

42
00:05:55,810 --> 00:06:00,720
And Mahakachana replies, friends, it is as though a man needing heartwood.

43
00:06:00,903 --> 00:06:17,487
seeking Heartwood, wandering in search of Heartwood, thought that Heartwood should be sought for among the branches and leaves of a great tree, standing possessed of Heartwood, after he had passed over the root and trunks.

44
00:06:18,028 --> 00:06:28,783
So it is with you, Venerable Sir, that you think that I should be asked about the meaning of this after you passed the Blessed One by, when you were face to face with the teacher.

45
00:06:29,641 --> 00:06:31,802
On knowing, the Blessed One knows.

46
00:06:32,303 --> 00:06:33,545
Seeing, He sees.

47
00:06:34,406 --> 00:06:35,107
He is vision.

48
00:06:35,226 --> 00:06:35,908
He is knowledge.

49
00:06:35,968 --> 00:06:36,848
He is the Dhamma.

50
00:06:37,168 --> 00:06:38,230
He is the Holy One.

51
00:06:38,350 --> 00:06:47,540
He is the Sayer, the Proclaimer, the Elucidator of Meaning, the Giver of the Deathless, the Lord of the Dhamma, Tatagata, the Transcendent One.

52
00:06:48,422 --> 00:06:51,444
That was the time when you should have asked the Blessed One the meaning.

53
00:06:52,545 --> 00:06:56,930
As I told you, and as He told you, you should have remembered it.

54
00:06:57,468 --> 00:06:59,670
So they say, well, of course this is true.

55
00:07:00,973 --> 00:07:03,817
There was that time and we would have remembered it.

56
00:07:04,278 --> 00:07:11,769
But Mahakachana is praised by the teacher and esteemed by his wise companions in the holy life.

57
00:07:11,889 --> 00:07:16,475
Let the venerable Mahakachana expound it without finding it troublesome.

58
00:07:17,898 --> 00:07:20,221
Okay, listen friends, says Mahakachana.

59
00:07:21,682 --> 00:07:27,170
Friends, when the Blessed One rose from his seat and went to his dwelling after giving this summary, so

60
00:07:27,454 --> 00:07:32,461
If you remember the statement, I'll just read it again because it's core to the discourse.

61
00:07:33,603 --> 00:07:53,326
As to the source through which perceptions and notions tinged by mental proliferation beset a man, if nothing is found there to delight him, welcome, hold on to, this is the underlying tendency of all unwholesome states.

62
00:07:54,809 --> 00:07:58,072
So now Mahakachana explains this.

63
00:07:58,093 --> 00:08:04,440
So some of it, hopefully you will grasp because we did do it in earlier discourses.

64
00:08:05,541 --> 00:08:09,627
Dependent on the I and forms, I consciousness arises.

65
00:08:10,528 --> 00:08:12,310
The meeting of the three is contact.

66
00:08:13,271 --> 00:08:15,714
With contact as condition, there is feeling.

67
00:08:16,776 --> 00:08:20,201
When one feels, that one perceives.

68
00:08:21,141 --> 00:08:24,545
What one perceives, that one thinks about.

69
00:08:25,302 --> 00:08:30,646
What one thinks about, that one mentally proliferates.

70
00:08:30,666 --> 00:08:48,302
With what one has mentally proliferated as the source, perceptions and notions tinged by mental proliferation beset a man with respect to the past, the future and present forms cognizable through the eye.

71
00:08:48,722 --> 00:08:53,807
So there's your process and I'm sure you'll recognize it very well.

72
00:08:55,070 --> 00:09:01,679
Having seen something, there comes a feeling whereby you say to yourself, this is pleasant or unpleasant.

73
00:09:01,700 --> 00:09:03,342
But there also is a perception.

74
00:09:03,923 --> 00:09:14,399
That perception will be an apperception, which means that it will fit into your past notions about what you're looking at.

75
00:09:15,081 --> 00:09:20,149
Then we think about it, and before we know it, we're off, we're proliferating.

76
00:09:20,736 --> 00:09:49,544
so um just take just take a simple example if you see somebody whom you had some problems with because that's always an easy one when you see them then there is of course not a very good feeling and your perception of them is added to your past perceptions of them so that's so that the concept of them is growing that process is called by the way a perception uh technically

77
00:09:49,626 --> 00:09:57,908
And then, of course, once you're into it, off the mind goes, thinking it's angry thoughts or it's anxious thoughts, worry thoughts, etc.

78
00:10:03,764 --> 00:10:04,345
One second.

79
00:10:07,042 --> 00:10:11,067
So now, of course, he goes through all the senses and, of course, through memories.

80
00:10:11,106 --> 00:10:14,149
That's the past and, of course, thinking about the future.

81
00:10:14,230 --> 00:10:28,706
So the mind constantly proliferates on a simple input from one of the six senses, something you've seen, heard, touched, et cetera, and something you remember or something that comes up into your mind.

82
00:10:30,609 --> 00:10:33,893
And then, of course, that's really sort of pointed out that

83
00:10:35,154 --> 00:10:41,283
that with eye consciousness, it is possible to point out the manifestation of contact.

84
00:10:41,803 --> 00:10:43,567
So all of these are connected.

85
00:10:43,886 --> 00:10:47,832
So you've got contact from contact feeling, from feeling perception.

86
00:10:47,873 --> 00:10:53,000
So in Buddhist psychology, whenever there's a feeling, a perception is concurrent.

87
00:10:53,059 --> 00:10:54,721
They both arise together.

88
00:10:55,423 --> 00:11:01,871
Perception with a feeling and a feeling with a perception, an idea, a photocopy, you could say.

89
00:11:02,527 --> 00:11:07,751
And with that perception, that points to a manifestation of thinking.

90
00:11:08,432 --> 00:11:17,600
Once there's thinking, then, of course, beset by these perceptions and notions, there is this mental proliferation.

91
00:11:17,740 --> 00:11:19,102
And off we go into fantasies.

92
00:11:21,004 --> 00:11:22,426
And then, of course, he says the opposite.

93
00:11:22,785 --> 00:11:32,414
If none of that happens, and remember, it's always based on underlying tendencies, unwholesome tendencies.

94
00:11:32,884 --> 00:11:44,580
So for instance, when the Buddha is asked a question, that question will always be just slightly different from all past questions, and it will demand some creative response.

95
00:11:45,360 --> 00:11:55,735
But he's not proliferating on wholesome tendencies that lie underneath his consciousness.

96
00:11:56,135 --> 00:12:01,001
He's coming from the heart of wisdom and the heart of compassion, and therefore,

97
00:12:01,336 --> 00:12:05,186
even though he's speaking, it wouldn't be called unwholesome.

98
00:12:05,206 --> 00:12:06,128
Okay?

99
00:12:06,148 --> 00:12:14,889
So we also can do that, but remember that always for us underlying everything we do is always this sense of self which mucks everything up.

100
00:12:18,159 --> 00:12:44,471
So just to state that again, so as to the source through which perceptions and notions tinged by mental proliferation be set about, if nothing is found there to delight in, so this is somebody who's liberated, nothing to delight in, to welcome, to hold on to, this is the end of the underlying tendency to lust, okay?

101
00:12:44,772 --> 00:12:50,639
To lust here means all greeds, aversions, views, conceit.

102
00:12:50,979 --> 00:12:51,639
That's a big one.

103
00:12:51,700 --> 00:12:53,562
Conceit doesn't go till the very end.

104
00:12:53,881 --> 00:12:58,548
The sense of I am and with it comparison, better than, worse than and so on.

105
00:12:59,469 --> 00:13:06,397
And it's the end of resorting to rods and weapons, quarrels, brawls, disputes, recrimination, malice and false speech.

106
00:13:07,076 --> 00:13:11,201
Here these evil and wholesome states cease without remainder.

107
00:13:11,451 --> 00:13:13,553
And that's why he says the world argues with me.

108
00:13:13,995 --> 00:13:15,495
I do not argue with the world.

109
00:13:15,777 --> 00:13:17,197
This is what the Buddha says of himself.

110
00:13:18,460 --> 00:13:25,006
Now, the Bhikkhus delighted and rejoiced in Venerable Kachana's work, and they rose to their seats and went to the Blessed One.

111
00:13:25,346 --> 00:13:31,234
And after they had paid the usual homage, they explained to him what Mahakachana had said.

112
00:13:31,793 --> 00:13:38,682
And the Blessed One says, Mahakachana is wise because Mahakachana has great wisdom.

113
00:13:39,403 --> 00:13:41,424
If you had asked me the meaning of this,

114
00:13:41,658 --> 00:13:46,462
I would have explained it to you in the same way that Mahakachana has explained it.

115
00:13:47,163 --> 00:13:50,907
Such is the meaning of this, and so you should remember it.

116
00:13:52,688 --> 00:13:55,552
Okay, so there we have this process.

117
00:13:56,111 --> 00:14:02,177
What the Buddha is pointing out is the process whereby we make things worse for ourselves.

118
00:14:02,479 --> 00:14:11,006
So remember, any unwholesome motivation, any unwholesome underlying tendency which rises into consciousness,

119
00:14:11,341 --> 00:14:19,779
As soon as it begins to proliferate as a fantasy or any sort of fantasy, then we are developing it.

120
00:14:19,941 --> 00:14:20,741
This is the point.

121
00:14:20,761 --> 00:14:21,804
We're developing it.

122
00:14:22,245 --> 00:14:25,111
So therefore, we have to be straight on the ball.

123
00:14:25,172 --> 00:14:28,539
As soon as we see we're dreaming, we're fantasizing about something.

124
00:14:29,025 --> 00:14:56,676
even if it looks rather good, you know, like saving the world from disaster and all that, you know, just to come off it, let it go and see if you can catch the underlying feeling, you know, any of these tendencies, the conceit, you know, to being great, to being famous, to being rich, to being powerful, there'll always be some underlying tendency there.

125
00:14:56,696 --> 00:14:58,677
And recognizing that,

126
00:14:59,535 --> 00:15:25,003
turning our attention towards it turning our attention towards it and and feeling it allowing it to manifest right is the way that that we heal the heart of these uh corruptions of these turbulences right they've got to come into consciousness somewhere or other right that's the purification process and that's of course what's happening when we practice vipassana

127
00:15:27,211 --> 00:15:37,467
So now, when this was all said, the Venerable Ananda, who, remember, is always with the Buddha for the last 20 years, is his assistant.

128
00:15:37,508 --> 00:15:48,386
Venerable Sir, just as a man, exhausted by hunger and weakness, came upon a honey bowl, in the course of eating it, he would find a sweet, delectable flavor.

129
00:15:48,888 --> 00:15:50,350
So too, Venerable Sir.

130
00:15:50,717 --> 00:16:16,741
any able-minded bhikkhu or meditator in the course of scrutinizing with wisdom the meaning of this discourse on the dharma would find satisfaction and confidence of mind venerable sir what is the name of this discourse of the dharma and the buddha replies as to that ananda you may remember that this discourse on the dharma as the honey ball discourse

131
00:16:17,195 --> 00:16:23,764
That is what the Blessed One said, and the Venerable Ananda was satisfied and delighted in the Buddha's words."

132
00:16:23,783 --> 00:16:29,772
Now, a honey bowl, if I can find out, here we are, just a minute.

133
00:16:30,852 --> 00:16:32,855
These are the ingredients of a honey bowl.

134
00:16:33,996 --> 00:16:38,984
Something like flour, ghee, molasses, honey, sugar, and so on.

135
00:16:39,364 --> 00:16:41,447
So it's definitely a honey bowl.

136
00:16:43,250 --> 00:16:49,678
So that's the teaching of this rather lovely little discourse, the honeyball discourse.

137
00:16:49,698 --> 00:16:51,821
Don't let the mind proliferate.

138
00:16:52,022 --> 00:16:54,684
And as soon as you do see it proliferating, come off it.

139
00:16:54,764 --> 00:16:55,265
It's a drug.

140
00:16:55,826 --> 00:17:04,157
And try to feel the underlying tendency, allowing it to rise and pass away is the way to purify the heart.

141
00:17:04,178 --> 00:17:09,644
I can only hope this rather lovely little discourse has inspired you.

142
00:17:09,929 --> 00:17:16,884
And that by your continuing practice, you will be liberated from all suffering sooner rather than later.

